{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 32, :comments ["<b>This is the law of… who has not…</b> The entire verse seems superfluous, and our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded (Torat Kohanim): ״זֹאת תּוֹרַת״ — that it enriched the possibility of a poor man bringing the offering of a rich man if he wishes. And if you say: why is a verse needed for this? Surely logic itself dictates thus, since the verse came only to lighten his burden, and if he wishes, a blessing may come upon him? It appears that since the verse wished to restrict a rich man — that if he brought the offering of a poor man he has not fulfilled his obligation, as they expounded from the word ״זֹאת״ as a restriction — one might have feared that the verse was strict about everything in what it restricted and said ״זֹאת״: a poor man in his poverty and a rich man in his wealth. Therefore Scripture says <b>תּוֹרַת</b> — teaching that a rich man may bring a poor man's offering if he wishes." "Yet it is still not known why the verse says <b>who has not the means to attain</b> etc. Perhaps it is because the verse was concerned lest a sage come and derive the law of insufficient means stated in the case of the metzora from the law of insufficient means stated in the case of the defilement of the Sanctuary and its offerings and the oath of imprecation etc. (Leviticus 5:7), where there too in the offering of extreme poverty, instead of two turtledoves — one for an olah and one for a chatat — they bring a tenth of an ephah. And he might derive from what is stated regarding the metzora that he too, if his means did not suffice for birds, should bring a tenth of an ephah. Therefore Scripture says ״זֹאת תּוֹרַת אֲשֶׁר וגו׳ אֲשֶׁר לֹא תַשִּׂיג יָדוֹ בְּטָהֳרָתוֹ״ — meaning, even though his means did not suffice for his purification that is specified, which are turtledoves etc., even so he must wriggle in the dust and die, and bring two turtledoves or two young pigeons specifically." "And by way of remez it is possible that the verse alluded to what they said in the holy Zohar (Tikkunim 22), that poverty is called tzaraat, and therefore both of them are called dead (Nedarim 64a). And this is what it alluded to in saying <b>in whom is the plague of tzaraat</b> — meaning, the plague is still in him. And what is the plague? He explained and said <b>who has not the means to attain in his purification</b> — meaning, his hand did not reach, on account of his poverty, to bring the offering required for his purification from his tzaraat, so that he needed to bring birds."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:56:50.162876367Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}